<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=armillary-sphere">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Science and discovery</p><h1 class="enc-title">Armillary sphere</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">3rd century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Armilla, Armil, Spherical astrolabe</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">An armillary sphere is a model of the sky made of rings that stand for the celestial equator, the ecliptic and other circles, centred on the Earth or the Sun. It was invented separately in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Hipparchus credited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eratosthenes">Eratosthenes</a> (276–194 BCE) with its invention, and Greek armillary spheres served as teaching tools in the 3rd century BCE. In China, Geng Shouchang introduced a fixed equatorial ring in 52 BCE, and Zhang Heng completed the sphere with horizon and meridian rings in 125 CE and turned it by water power. Islamic and medieval European astronomers took it up, and it remained the main instrument for finding celestial positions until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telescope">telescope</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/armillary-sphere.jpg" alt="Armillary sphere" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: physical object: Jost Bürgi, Antonius Eisenhoit Photograph: Chris Bainbridge · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Armillary_sphere_with_astronomical_clock.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-needham">Joseph Needham</a> traced the earliest armillary sphere to the astronomers Shi Shen and Gan De in the 4th century BCE, who had a primitive single-ring instrument, but the sinologist Christopher Cullen rejects that dating and places the beginnings in the 1st century BCE. Under the Western Han, Geng Shouchang introduced the first permanently fixed equatorial ring in 52 BCE, and under the Eastern Han Fu An and Jia Kui added the ecliptic ring by 84 CE. Zhang Heng completed the sphere with horizon and meridian rings in 125 CE and operated it with an inflow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/water-clock">water clock</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/su-song">Su Song</a>'s clock tower of 1094 was crowned by an armillary sphere.</p><p>The Greek astronomer Hipparchus credited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eratosthenes">Eratosthenes</a> with the invention. Armillary spheres existed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a> and served as teaching tools in the 3rd century BCE, though the fully developed sphere of nine circles perhaps did not exist until the mid-2nd century CE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>An armillary sphere is a model of objects on the celestial sphere: a spherical framework of rings centred on Earth or the Sun, representing lines of the celestial coordinate system and features such as the ecliptic. It differs from a celestial globe, a smooth sphere meant mainly to map the constellations. The English name comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> armilla, 'circle' or 'bracelet'. In its simplest form it is a ring in the plane of the equator, crossed by a second ring in the plane of the meridian. With the Earth at the centre it is called Ptolemaic, and with the Sun at the centre, Copernican.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Islamic astronomers continued to build and improve armillary spheres, and the spherical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astrolabe">astrolabe</a>, a variant, was likely invented in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>; the earliest description is by the Persian astronomer Nayrizi. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, Chang Yŏngsil built one in 1433 for King Sejong. Before the European telescope of the 17th century, the armillary sphere was the prime instrument of astronomers for determining celestial positions. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> scientists and public figures had themselves painted with a hand on one. It became a badge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-i-of-portugal">Manuel I of Portugal</a> and a symbol of Portugal and its overseas empire, and it appears on the national flag.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Armillary sphere” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>3rd century BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Ancient Greece</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/encyclopedia/eratosthenes">Eratosthenes</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Also arose independently</span>China (1st century BCE)</p><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Joseph Needham traced the Chinese armillary sphere to the single-ring instrument of Shi Shen and Gan De in the 4th century BCE, a dating that Christopher Cullen rejects.</p><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=armillary-sphere">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
